1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:36,370 --> 00:00:38,305
♪♪

3
00:00:49,349 --> 00:00:51,375
♪♪

4
00:01:04,431 --> 00:01:06,309
- Quiet, please.
- A-mark.

5
00:01:06,333 --> 00:01:08,325
Why don't we get
him to smile...?

6
00:01:11,572 --> 00:01:15,318
And... action.

7
00:01:15,342 --> 00:01:19,188
The impact of the crash
threw me to the floor of the bus.

8
00:01:19,212 --> 00:01:21,010
No good. Cut, cut.

9
00:01:21,982 --> 00:01:24,627
A-cam.

10
00:01:24,651 --> 00:01:26,984
A-cam four.

11
00:01:28,989 --> 00:01:30,967
Okay, here we go, and action.

12
00:01:30,991 --> 00:01:35,271
The impact of the crash
threw me to the floor of the bus.

13
00:01:35,295 --> 00:01:37,006
- Cut, cut.
- Line, line.

14
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:38,708
A-cam.

15
00:01:38,732 --> 00:01:41,077
One, take four.

16
00:01:41,101 --> 00:01:43,212
Get ready, single.

17
00:01:43,236 --> 00:01:45,815
Action.

18
00:01:45,839 --> 00:01:50,386
The impact of the crash
threw me to the floor of the bus,

19
00:01:50,410 --> 00:01:53,189
causing permanent
and painful injuries

20
00:01:53,213 --> 00:01:57,207
to my neck and my back.

21
00:01:58,452 --> 00:02:02,899
I was unable to work
and support my family.

22
00:02:02,923 --> 00:02:04,767
All hope was gone...

23
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:08,605
until my wife suggested
I talk to a lawyer,

24
00:02:08,629 --> 00:02:11,107
so I did.

25
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:14,644
Now my future is bright...

26
00:02:14,668 --> 00:02:17,001
thanks to this man here...

27
00:02:18,038 --> 00:02:20,507
Eugene Dobbs.

28
00:02:22,843 --> 00:02:24,821
Thank you, Clayton.

29
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:26,656
Friend, are you
like Mr. Sadowski,

30
00:02:26,680 --> 00:02:29,826
a victim of someone
else's negligence?

31
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:32,395
Have you suffered
bodily harm, anguish,

32
00:02:32,419 --> 00:02:33,863
lost wages?

33
00:02:33,887 --> 00:02:37,533
Are bill collectors
banging at your door?

34
00:02:37,557 --> 00:02:40,570
We at Wheeler &
Dobbs know how you feel,

35
00:02:40,594 --> 00:02:44,073
and we are ready to help
you get what you deserve,

36
00:02:44,097 --> 00:02:45,575
like the $2 million we got...

37
00:02:45,599 --> 00:02:48,478
Damn it, Eugene, I told
you we can't say "$2 million"!

38
00:02:48,502 --> 00:02:50,279
Cut! Mr. Wheeler?

39
00:02:50,303 --> 00:02:52,315
We only got him $200,000.

40
00:02:52,339 --> 00:02:54,584
He was awarded
$2 million, Nolan.

41
00:02:54,608 --> 00:02:57,153
After the city appealed,
he only got $200,000.

42
00:02:57,177 --> 00:03:00,156
If you don't like the way I
do the commercial, you do it.

43
00:03:00,180 --> 00:03:01,978
I can't.

44
00:03:03,283 --> 00:03:05,762
You know I can't.

45
00:03:05,786 --> 00:03:07,830
Then butt out.

46
00:03:09,823 --> 00:03:12,101
Okay, folks, uh,
this will be a pickup.

47
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:13,970
Ready?

48
00:03:13,994 --> 00:03:15,371
Roll 'em.

49
00:03:15,395 --> 00:03:17,807
And... action.

50
00:03:17,831 --> 00:03:20,643
We at Wheeler & Dobbs
aren't just attorneys.

51
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:22,979
We're your friends.

52
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:26,549
In your time of
need, we'll be there

53
00:03:26,573 --> 00:03:29,407
and we won't let you down.

54
00:03:31,545 --> 00:03:32,877
A-sticks.

55
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:44,434
Now, remember, this is a
pretrial settlement conference.

56
00:03:44,458 --> 00:03:45,435
It's not a hearing.

57
00:03:45,459 --> 00:03:47,804
You just sit there and relax

58
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:49,639
while I answer the
judge's questions.

59
00:03:49,663 --> 00:03:51,574
In an hour, I'll be
taking you to lunch.

60
00:03:51,598 --> 00:03:53,109
So the judge is gonna decide

61
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:55,311
whether I'm gonna get all
this money you're asking for?

62
00:03:55,335 --> 00:03:56,946
I'll tell him your
side of the story,

63
00:03:56,970 --> 00:03:58,581
then the DeMont
company lawyer will tell him

64
00:03:58,605 --> 00:03:59,949
their side of the story.

65
00:03:59,973 --> 00:04:02,018
Then the judge will decide
how much money you'd get

66
00:04:02,042 --> 00:04:03,419
if you went to trial,

67
00:04:03,443 --> 00:04:06,489
and then DeMont's lawyer
is going to rush over to me

68
00:04:06,513 --> 00:04:08,825
and offer us a settlement.

69
00:04:08,849 --> 00:04:12,161
Believe me, DeMont
does not want to go to trial.

70
00:04:12,185 --> 00:04:14,030
You mean, this whole
thing could be over soon?

71
00:04:14,054 --> 00:04:15,732
John, with any luck,
by the end of the day,

72
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:20,269
you are going to be a
millionaire several times over.

73
00:04:20,293 --> 00:04:21,304
Aha, there you are.

74
00:04:21,328 --> 00:04:22,371
Dr. McNeeley.

75
00:04:22,395 --> 00:04:23,673
How you doing?

76
00:04:23,697 --> 00:04:27,043
Well, I've, uh, just, uh,
reexamined your latest X-rays,

77
00:04:27,067 --> 00:04:29,779
and I'm sorry to say that in
addition to everything else,

78
00:04:29,803 --> 00:04:32,582
you've sustained a hairline
fracture to your left clavicle.

79
00:04:32,606 --> 00:04:36,018
Funny, collarbone's the only
part of me that doesn't hurt.

80
00:04:36,042 --> 00:04:37,653
You gonna be at the
conference, too, Doc?

81
00:04:37,677 --> 00:04:39,889
No, I can't, but,
uh, I'll be here

82
00:04:39,913 --> 00:04:42,125
if Eugene needs
to confer with me.

83
00:04:42,149 --> 00:04:43,326
Good luck.

84
00:04:43,350 --> 00:04:45,717
Thanks a lot.

85
00:04:57,831 --> 00:04:59,976
Well, well, well, Dr. McNeeley.

86
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,912
Still chasing ambulances
with Eugene Dobbs?

87
00:05:02,936 --> 00:05:04,781
Some things never change.

88
00:05:04,805 --> 00:05:06,273
This way.

89
00:05:09,009 --> 00:05:13,022
Uh, am I correct in assuming
that the guardrail that gave way

90
00:05:13,046 --> 00:05:15,825
and caused Mr. Kramer's
fall was inspected

91
00:05:15,849 --> 00:05:17,860
at the same time as
the ride it surrounded?

92
00:05:17,884 --> 00:05:18,995
Yes, sir, that is correct.

93
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:20,997
Excuse me, Your Honor.

94
00:05:21,021 --> 00:05:22,932
That guardrail was
<i>supposed</i> to be inspected,

95
00:05:22,956 --> 00:05:24,734
but I have depositions
from park employees

96
00:05:24,758 --> 00:05:25,802
that say that it wasn't.

97
00:05:25,826 --> 00:05:27,537
My client cannot be blamed

98
00:05:27,561 --> 00:05:30,106
for the negligence
of state officials.

99
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:33,109
Al DeMont spends
thousands of dollars every year

100
00:05:33,133 --> 00:05:36,245
advertising that his
water park is safe,

101
00:05:36,269 --> 00:05:39,482
and hardworking families with
innocent children believe him.

102
00:05:39,506 --> 00:05:42,018
And they spend millions at your
park, don't they, Mr. DeMont?

103
00:05:42,042 --> 00:05:43,019
Your Honor...

104
00:05:43,043 --> 00:05:45,478
All right, that's
enough, Mr. Dobbs.

105
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,626
Now, I see that your
expert medical witness

106
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:52,628
is Dr. Eldon McNeeley.

107
00:05:52,652 --> 00:05:53,763
Expert.

108
00:05:53,787 --> 00:05:56,599
Dr. McNeeley is the
distinguished holder

109
00:05:56,623 --> 00:05:58,334
of the Fontmeyer
Chair in Orthopedics

110
00:05:58,358 --> 00:05:59,669
at Robertson Hospital.

111
00:05:59,693 --> 00:06:02,738
Yes, yes, I know, and,
uh, you, Miss Radovich,

112
00:06:02,762 --> 00:06:05,775
intend to put Dr. James
Liu on the stand.

113
00:06:05,799 --> 00:06:08,711
Yes, sir, and his opinion
of Mr. Kramer's injury

114
00:06:08,735 --> 00:06:10,947
is radically different from
that of Dr. McNeeley's.

115
00:06:10,971 --> 00:06:13,049
Mm-hmm, mm-hmm.

116
00:06:13,073 --> 00:06:17,620
Now, what's this
about lost wages?

117
00:06:17,644 --> 00:06:20,790
My client was a college
football coach, Your Honor.

118
00:06:20,814 --> 00:06:23,559
Like the great Vince Lombardi,
he was an active, physical man.

119
00:06:23,583 --> 00:06:26,095
Like Rockne, Parseghian,
Lou Holtz, Eddie Robinson,

120
00:06:26,119 --> 00:06:28,350
coaching was his life.

121
00:06:29,389 --> 00:06:32,368
But now, due to
constant pain and agony,

122
00:06:32,392 --> 00:06:33,769
this shell of a man...

123
00:06:33,793 --> 00:06:37,907
I get the idea, Mr. Dobbs,
but since when does the coach

124
00:06:37,931 --> 00:06:41,043
of a state junior
college football team

125
00:06:41,067 --> 00:06:43,880
earn $6 million?

126
00:06:43,904 --> 00:06:45,615
That's potential, Your Honor.

127
00:06:45,639 --> 00:06:47,283
At the time of
my client's injury,

128
00:06:47,307 --> 00:06:50,920
he was being pursued
by five major universities.

129
00:06:50,944 --> 00:06:52,555
He would have
accepted one of them,

130
00:06:52,579 --> 00:06:54,223
then gone with the
pros and made millions.

131
00:06:54,247 --> 00:06:55,992
You are lying.

132
00:06:56,016 --> 00:06:58,461
Those schools were
not pursuing him;

133
00:06:58,485 --> 00:07:00,463
he was pursuing those schools.

134
00:07:00,487 --> 00:07:03,833
Your Honor, his client
initiated those job interviews

135
00:07:03,857 --> 00:07:05,268
because he was
afraid he was about

136
00:07:05,292 --> 00:07:06,636
to be fired.

137
00:07:06,660 --> 00:07:08,304
You can't prove that.

138
00:07:08,328 --> 00:07:09,572
The hell, I can't.

139
00:07:09,596 --> 00:07:10,940
Is this true, Mr. Dobbs?

140
00:07:10,964 --> 00:07:13,009
Is what true?

141
00:07:13,033 --> 00:07:17,368
That your client initiated those
interviews and not vice versa?

142
00:07:19,406 --> 00:07:22,968
Mr. Dobbs, answer me.

143
00:07:25,879 --> 00:07:30,026
So Mr. Kramer made the first
calls, but they returned them.

144
00:07:30,050 --> 00:07:32,595
Mr. Dobbs, this
is reprehensible.

145
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:35,431
Once more, you've
misrepresented the facts.

146
00:07:35,455 --> 00:07:39,535
I have no doubt that you
were injured, Mr. Kramer,

147
00:07:39,559 --> 00:07:42,505
but no matter what your
attorney has led you to believe,

148
00:07:42,529 --> 00:07:44,974
your settlement will be modest.

149
00:07:44,998 --> 00:07:48,799
There'll be just enough money
to cover your medical bills.

150
00:07:49,803 --> 00:07:51,580
We're done here.

151
00:07:51,604 --> 00:07:53,903
Good day.

152
00:07:58,912 --> 00:08:01,691
That's why we call this
judge "Tightwad Artie,"

153
00:08:01,715 --> 00:08:05,661
but he is not a jury,
thank the Lord above.

154
00:08:05,685 --> 00:08:08,154
Juries listen with their hearts.

155
00:08:09,189 --> 00:08:11,167
What's your offer?

156
00:08:11,191 --> 00:08:13,436
Forget it, Dobbs.

157
00:08:13,460 --> 00:08:16,205
Contrary to what you may
think, juries are very smart,

158
00:08:16,229 --> 00:08:18,374
and I want this one
to see you in action.

159
00:08:18,398 --> 00:08:19,475
Because when they hear

160
00:08:19,499 --> 00:08:21,444
how you blow everything
out of proportion

161
00:08:21,468 --> 00:08:23,713
and they watch that trained
seal of an MD out there

162
00:08:23,737 --> 00:08:24,847
perform on the stand,

163
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:26,716
they are going to
resent the hell out of you,

164
00:08:26,740 --> 00:08:29,118
and your client
will be very lucky

165
00:08:29,142 --> 00:08:30,987
to walk away with half a cent.

166
00:08:31,011 --> 00:08:33,674
See you in court, gentlemen.

167
00:08:38,485 --> 00:08:40,062
John, wait.

168
00:08:40,086 --> 00:08:41,364
John?

169
00:08:41,388 --> 00:08:44,133
Come on.

170
00:08:44,157 --> 00:08:46,535
John, don't worry.

171
00:08:46,559 --> 00:08:47,870
Everything's going to be okay.

172
00:08:47,894 --> 00:08:49,005
Okay?

173
00:08:49,029 --> 00:08:50,406
I heard what the
hell she said in there.

174
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:52,375
You put me through all
this garbage for nothing.

175
00:08:52,399 --> 00:08:53,376
What's going on?

176
00:08:53,400 --> 00:08:56,746
John, she is wrong, believe me.

177
00:08:56,770 --> 00:08:59,115
See you.

178
00:08:59,139 --> 00:09:00,573
Shall we?

179
00:09:01,608 --> 00:09:03,085
You know what I think?

180
00:09:03,109 --> 00:09:05,388
You're a lying, thieving jerk.

181
00:09:05,412 --> 00:09:07,490
You said my
family'd be set for life.

182
00:09:07,514 --> 00:09:09,025
And they will be, John.

183
00:09:09,049 --> 00:09:10,192
Just calm down.

184
00:09:10,216 --> 00:09:12,128
Hey, hey, hey, what's going on?

185
00:09:12,152 --> 00:09:14,397
The settlement conference
wasn't entirely successful,

186
00:09:14,421 --> 00:09:17,199
and John is just a
little upset, that's all.

187
00:09:17,223 --> 00:09:19,001
It was in the bag.
What went wrong?

188
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:20,302
It was the lost wages thing.

189
00:09:20,326 --> 00:09:21,437
We went over that.

190
00:09:21,461 --> 00:09:22,838
Didn't you say what
I told you to say?

191
00:09:22,862 --> 00:09:24,774
He lied to 'em, just
like he lied to me.

192
00:09:24,798 --> 00:09:27,710
John, I didn't lie to you.

193
00:09:27,734 --> 00:09:29,178
You'll get your money.

194
00:09:29,202 --> 00:09:31,380
This thing isn't over,
not by a long shot.

195
00:09:31,404 --> 00:09:32,715
My body is a wreck,

196
00:09:32,739 --> 00:09:34,817
my career's ruined, and
because you get greedy,

197
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:36,685
my family is gonna
wind up with nothing.

198
00:09:36,709 --> 00:09:41,424
John, look... we have
a lot of options left.

199
00:09:41,448 --> 00:09:43,292
You come to my office
tonight and we'll talk.

200
00:09:43,316 --> 00:09:44,693
Okay, 7:00?

201
00:09:44,717 --> 00:09:47,163
John?

202
00:09:47,187 --> 00:09:51,400
Nobody is going to
wind up on welfare here,

203
00:09:51,424 --> 00:09:52,668
I promise you.

204
00:09:52,692 --> 00:09:53,887
Okay?

205
00:09:55,929 --> 00:09:57,955
Okay.

206
00:09:59,666 --> 00:10:01,794
- Damn it, Eugene.
- Oh, shut up.

207
00:10:04,237 --> 00:10:06,115
Why can't you just
do what I tell you to?

208
00:10:06,139 --> 00:10:08,217
- Oh, please. Will you stop?
- No, no "please."

209
00:10:42,342 --> 00:10:45,335
♪♪

210
00:12:04,624 --> 00:12:07,303
Says here you had an argument

211
00:12:07,327 --> 00:12:09,471
with Eugene Dobbs
yesterday afternoon.

212
00:12:09,495 --> 00:12:11,874
He made me think
I'd make a fortune.

213
00:12:11,898 --> 00:12:14,410
But I'll be lucky to
cover my medical bills.

214
00:12:14,434 --> 00:12:17,446
And the police found the murder
weapon in your motel room.

215
00:12:17,470 --> 00:12:19,381
I don't know how it got there.

216
00:12:19,405 --> 00:12:21,016
Hmm.

217
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:24,653
The janitor at the
office building said

218
00:12:24,677 --> 00:12:27,389
that he saw a man
fitting your description

219
00:12:27,413 --> 00:12:30,059
going into Eugene
Dobbs' office last night.

220
00:12:30,083 --> 00:12:31,794
No, I didn't go to his office.

221
00:12:31,818 --> 00:12:33,762
He called the motel
and told me to meet him

222
00:12:33,786 --> 00:12:37,314
at a sushi place on Peachtree,
but he never showed.

223
00:12:39,359 --> 00:12:41,403
I didn't kill him, Mr. Matlock.

224
00:12:41,427 --> 00:12:42,771
I swear.

225
00:12:42,795 --> 00:12:47,142
Well, I guess we know
why he never showed.

226
00:12:47,166 --> 00:12:49,612
Who else knew
about this meeting?

227
00:12:49,636 --> 00:12:51,180
Everybody who heard us arguing.

228
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:54,483
Uh, Nolan Wheeler,
Dr. McNeeley, Al DeMont.

229
00:12:54,507 --> 00:12:56,252
They were all listening.

230
00:12:56,276 --> 00:12:58,787
Well, that's a start.

231
00:12:58,811 --> 00:13:02,077
Is that cane, uh, temporary?

232
00:13:03,116 --> 00:13:04,627
No.

233
00:13:04,651 --> 00:13:06,517
This is as good
as I'll ever get.

234
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:09,398
Hmm.

235
00:13:09,422 --> 00:13:12,701
Well, I'll go bail you out.

236
00:13:12,725 --> 00:13:15,170
How am I going to pay you?

237
00:13:17,096 --> 00:13:20,909
Well... a pro bono
case every now and then

238
00:13:20,933 --> 00:13:22,544
is good for the spirit.

239
00:13:22,568 --> 00:13:25,347
And besides, I can't
let you go around

240
00:13:25,371 --> 00:13:27,316
thinking all lawyers are...

241
00:13:27,340 --> 00:13:30,152
are like the late,
unlamented Eugene Dobbs.

242
00:13:52,398 --> 00:13:54,343
Oh, Les, come on in.

243
00:13:54,367 --> 00:13:55,744
Have some coffee.

244
00:13:55,768 --> 00:13:56,845
There's an extra cup.

245
00:13:56,869 --> 00:13:58,781
But don't be
offended if I leave.

246
00:13:58,805 --> 00:14:00,916
Oh. You in a rush?

247
00:14:00,940 --> 00:14:03,252
Yeah, I'm involved in a case

248
00:14:03,276 --> 00:14:06,322
dealing with an
ambulance-chasing lawyer.

249
00:14:06,346 --> 00:14:08,791
Boy, those guys are
all over, aren't they?

250
00:14:08,815 --> 00:14:09,958
Yeah.

251
00:14:09,982 --> 00:14:11,994
I'm surprised there
wasn't one around

252
00:14:12,018 --> 00:14:14,596
the other day when
I got sideswiped.

253
00:14:14,620 --> 00:14:15,764
Yeah?

254
00:14:15,788 --> 00:14:17,166
Any damage? You get hurt?

255
00:14:17,190 --> 00:14:18,600
No, it's nothing.

256
00:14:18,624 --> 00:14:20,869
Well, that's what
these fellas do...

257
00:14:20,893 --> 00:14:24,306
Make something out of nothing...
Especially in a sideswipe case.

258
00:14:24,330 --> 00:14:28,911
Why, some of them get their
clients thousands of dollars.

259
00:14:28,935 --> 00:14:30,779
They can?

260
00:14:30,803 --> 00:14:32,314
Oh, yeah.

261
00:14:32,338 --> 00:14:34,616
I heard of one case like yours.

262
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:37,769
This lawyer got his
client a half million bucks.

263
00:14:38,811 --> 00:14:40,155
A half a million?

264
00:14:40,179 --> 00:14:42,080
Mm-hmm.

265
00:14:43,950 --> 00:14:45,961
I wonder if maybe I shouldn't

266
00:14:45,985 --> 00:14:48,853
have had my neck
checked after it happened.

267
00:14:49,889 --> 00:14:51,100
Does it hurt?

268
00:14:51,124 --> 00:14:53,469
Uh, well, they say
that sometimes

269
00:14:53,493 --> 00:14:57,039
whiplash doesn't show
up for a few days after.

270
00:14:57,063 --> 00:15:00,209
That's why you have to report
these accidents right away.

271
00:15:00,233 --> 00:15:02,759
If-If you wait too long,
you don't have a case.

272
00:15:04,971 --> 00:15:07,116
Yeah.

273
00:15:07,140 --> 00:15:09,318
Well, I'd better go, Ben.

274
00:15:09,342 --> 00:15:10,986
Uh, but don't... don't
you want your coffee?

275
00:15:11,010 --> 00:15:13,856
Uh... uh... uh, no, Ben.

276
00:15:13,880 --> 00:15:16,247
I'll-I'll-I'll... I'll
see you later.

277
00:15:33,599 --> 00:15:35,363
Ah, hi, Bob.

278
00:15:38,070 --> 00:15:40,816
Mr. Dobbs did all right
for himself, didn't he?

279
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:43,385
Yeah. Ever see any
of his TV commercials?

280
00:15:43,409 --> 00:15:45,421
Sold himself like corn flakes.

281
00:15:45,445 --> 00:15:47,623
"Eugene Dobbs,
your friend in need."

282
00:15:47,647 --> 00:15:49,158
Yeah, I've seen
them. I've seen them.

283
00:15:49,182 --> 00:15:50,559
- Is that his office?
- Yeah.

284
00:15:50,583 --> 00:15:52,895
His widow's in there now
picking up a few personal items.

285
00:15:52,919 --> 00:15:54,730
I guess she's pretty upset.

286
00:15:54,754 --> 00:15:56,064
You could say that.

287
00:15:56,088 --> 00:15:57,733
Mm-hmm.

288
00:16:01,360 --> 00:16:03,372
You clumsy moron!

289
00:16:03,396 --> 00:16:04,673
- Sorry, ma'am.
- Sorry?

290
00:16:04,697 --> 00:16:05,741
You klutz!

291
00:16:05,765 --> 00:16:08,234
This vase cost $2,000.

292
00:16:11,103 --> 00:16:13,115
Uh, uh, Mrs. Dobbs?

293
00:16:13,139 --> 00:16:14,383
Hi.

294
00:16:14,407 --> 00:16:16,051
Uh, I'm Ben
Matlock. I'm a lawyer.

295
00:16:16,075 --> 00:16:17,619
Good. Sue the Police Department.

296
00:16:17,643 --> 00:16:19,755
These bozos
destroyed my property.

297
00:16:19,779 --> 00:16:23,125
Oh. Mr. Dobbs made a
lot of money, didn't he?

298
00:16:23,149 --> 00:16:25,561
- You could say that.
- Wheeler & Dobbs

299
00:16:25,585 --> 00:16:27,796
were the hottest team
in personal litigation.

300
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:29,465
Oh, they were.

301
00:16:29,489 --> 00:16:32,367
But Eugene was the whole
show, if you know what I mean.

302
00:16:32,391 --> 00:16:34,703
Last week, he told me he
was going to dump Nolan.

303
00:16:34,727 --> 00:16:37,606
Oh, did-did-did...
Did he say why?

304
00:16:37,630 --> 00:16:38,941
He didn't have to.

305
00:16:38,965 --> 00:16:41,210
With Eugene, everything
was about money.

306
00:16:41,234 --> 00:16:43,779
Oh, not that I have
anything against money.

307
00:16:43,803 --> 00:16:46,315
I can spend it with
the best of them.

308
00:16:53,045 --> 00:16:54,957
That's going to
cost you, Einstein.

309
00:16:54,981 --> 00:16:56,916
That is going to cost you.

310
00:17:05,892 --> 00:17:10,739
Your Honor, due
to-to the unforeseen

311
00:17:10,763 --> 00:17:15,744
ter-termination of
the par-participation

312
00:17:15,768 --> 00:17:19,882
of co-counsel for the
plaintiff, Ms. Elaine Davison,

313
00:17:19,906 --> 00:17:23,252
we would ask
the court to cons...

314
00:17:23,276 --> 00:17:26,075
You want a continuance
because Mr. Dobbs is dead, right?

315
00:17:27,446 --> 00:17:29,558
That-That would be
correct, Your Honor, yes.

316
00:17:29,582 --> 00:17:32,794
Now, this courtroom,
9:00, Friday the 28th.

317
00:17:32,818 --> 00:17:35,264
Uh, next time, just
spit it out, Mr. Wheeler.

318
00:17:36,389 --> 00:17:38,381
Next case.

319
00:17:42,762 --> 00:17:46,475
Uh, Your Honor, in the
matter of Lock versus Parson...

320
00:17:46,499 --> 00:17:48,277
Mr. Wheeler?

321
00:17:48,301 --> 00:17:50,078
Hello. My name is
Michelle Thomas.

322
00:17:50,102 --> 00:17:51,280
I work with Ben Matlock.

323
00:17:51,304 --> 00:17:52,848
Could I ask you a few questions?

324
00:17:52,872 --> 00:17:54,898
Sure, but outside.

325
00:17:57,176 --> 00:17:59,254
Courtrooms make you nervous?

326
00:17:59,278 --> 00:18:00,923
I break into a sweat

327
00:18:00,947 --> 00:18:02,758
just driving past
the courthouse.

328
00:18:04,417 --> 00:18:05,861
There.

329
00:18:05,885 --> 00:18:08,397
My heart finally
stopped pounding.

330
00:18:08,421 --> 00:18:10,332
What a handicap for a lawyer.

331
00:18:10,356 --> 00:18:12,000
I'm a damn good one, too.

332
00:18:12,024 --> 00:18:13,902
So long as you don't
have to stand up and speak

333
00:18:13,926 --> 00:18:16,521
in front of a bunch
of strangers?

334
00:18:18,331 --> 00:18:20,095
Um, let's walk.

335
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:23,579
So, that's why you teamed
up with Eugene Dobbs.

336
00:18:23,603 --> 00:18:24,813
He was your front.

337
00:18:24,837 --> 00:18:27,316
He was a brilliant
orator... A star.

338
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:29,017
He lived for the spotlight.

339
00:18:29,041 --> 00:18:31,186
Unfortunately, he didn't
have a brain in his head.

340
00:18:31,210 --> 00:18:32,321
Oh?

341
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:34,122
I was Edgar Bergen;
he was Charlie McCarthy.

342
00:18:34,146 --> 00:18:35,457
I'd tell him what to say,

343
00:18:35,481 --> 00:18:37,326
he'd say it in court, and
juries would applaud.

344
00:18:37,350 --> 00:18:38,994
Together, we were
the perfect lawyer...

345
00:18:39,018 --> 00:18:40,362
Brains and blarney.

346
00:18:40,386 --> 00:18:42,898
Then why was he thinking
about leaving the firm?

347
00:18:42,922 --> 00:18:45,033
Uh, who told you that?

348
00:18:45,057 --> 00:18:46,501
His wife.

349
00:18:46,525 --> 00:18:47,769
Some wife.

350
00:18:47,793 --> 00:18:50,024
More like a hood ornament.

351
00:18:52,331 --> 00:18:54,142
Eugene got cocky.

352
00:18:54,166 --> 00:18:56,211
Started thinking he
didn't need me, after all.

353
00:18:56,235 --> 00:18:58,113
Like I said, he was stupid.

354
00:18:58,137 --> 00:18:59,982
His leaving would have
ruined your business.

355
00:19:00,006 --> 00:19:02,050
Well, his murder wasn't
exactly a shot in the arm.

356
00:19:02,074 --> 00:19:03,819
But at least with him
dead, you get to collect

357
00:19:03,843 --> 00:19:05,621
on that million-dollar
insurance policy

358
00:19:05,645 --> 00:19:07,956
you took out on
each other, don't you?

359
00:19:07,980 --> 00:19:10,192
Uh, I'm a suspect?

360
00:19:10,216 --> 00:19:12,060
Well, you did know
he'd be having a meeting

361
00:19:12,084 --> 00:19:13,895
in his office that night
with John Kramer.

362
00:19:13,919 --> 00:19:15,864
So what? I was at home.

363
00:19:15,888 --> 00:19:17,432
Alone?

364
00:19:17,456 --> 00:19:18,634
Yes.

365
00:19:22,194 --> 00:19:24,006
My stomach's killing me.

366
00:19:24,030 --> 00:19:25,040
I'm due in court.

367
00:19:25,064 --> 00:19:26,041
Where's my pills?

368
00:19:26,065 --> 00:19:27,226
Look, I didn't kill him!

369
00:19:29,835 --> 00:19:31,146
Hey, Nolan.

370
00:19:31,170 --> 00:19:33,181
- Leave me alone.
- Hey, Nolan.

371
00:19:33,205 --> 00:19:34,750
- Nolan.
- Just get away from me.

372
00:19:34,774 --> 00:19:36,218
Hey, Nolan, I'll
get away from you.

373
00:19:36,242 --> 00:19:37,686
- Just give me five minutes.
- I don't want to talk to you.

374
00:19:37,710 --> 00:19:39,121
- I don't know you.
- You don't want to...

375
00:19:39,145 --> 00:19:41,223
- Look...
- Just don't call me again.

376
00:19:41,247 --> 00:19:42,524
I'm not going to call you again.

377
00:19:42,548 --> 00:19:44,107
- I...
- Leave me alone.

378
00:19:48,521 --> 00:19:50,432
Look out below!

379
00:20:03,402 --> 00:20:05,803
Hey, watch it! Come on.

380
00:20:16,048 --> 00:20:18,827
Yeah, first time I ever
went to a water park,

381
00:20:18,851 --> 00:20:21,129
when it was time
to leave, I threw a fit.

382
00:20:21,153 --> 00:20:22,798
My father had to carry me out.

383
00:20:22,822 --> 00:20:24,166
And now you own it.

384
00:20:24,190 --> 00:20:26,068
I wouldn't mind owning
something like this.

385
00:20:26,092 --> 00:20:27,602
Yeah, nobody can carry me out.

386
00:20:27,626 --> 00:20:29,304
- It's all mine.
- Yeah.

387
00:20:29,328 --> 00:20:33,408
Uh, I hear you've had quite
a few personal injury cases

388
00:20:33,432 --> 00:20:35,977
brought against you in
the past couple years.

389
00:20:36,001 --> 00:20:37,612
Oh, yeah. Anytime you have

390
00:20:37,636 --> 00:20:39,948
this amount of people
in a place like this,

391
00:20:39,972 --> 00:20:42,084
you're bound to
have some accidents.

392
00:20:42,108 --> 00:20:43,819
That's why they
invented insurance.

393
00:20:43,843 --> 00:20:45,220
Yeah.

394
00:20:45,244 --> 00:20:46,955
12, though... that's a lot.

395
00:20:46,979 --> 00:20:49,991
A lot, and all brought
by Eugene Dobbs.

396
00:20:50,015 --> 00:20:53,028
Well, they were
mostly nuisance cases.

397
00:20:53,052 --> 00:20:54,029
Fight 'em?

398
00:20:54,053 --> 00:20:57,032
Just cost me a lot of
time and not much money.

399
00:20:57,056 --> 00:20:58,767
- Yeah, I did fight 'em.
- Yeah. Yeah.

400
00:20:58,791 --> 00:21:02,404
Well, nuisance or not, I
bet your insurance went up.

401
00:21:02,428 --> 00:21:04,172
Didn't get any lower.

402
00:21:04,196 --> 00:21:06,875
Is that why you filed that, uh,

403
00:21:06,899 --> 00:21:09,077
harassment complaint,
uh, against him

404
00:21:09,101 --> 00:21:10,779
at the State Bar Association?

405
00:21:10,803 --> 00:21:12,080
Well, I can see you're better

406
00:21:12,104 --> 00:21:14,149
at doing your homework
than I ever was.

407
00:21:14,173 --> 00:21:16,384
Yes, I did file a
complaint against him,

408
00:21:16,408 --> 00:21:19,221
and he filed a
counter-complaint against me.

409
00:21:19,245 --> 00:21:21,890
And we both got smart
and called a truce.

410
00:21:21,914 --> 00:21:23,692
And we both agreed to back off

411
00:21:23,716 --> 00:21:25,227
until the Kramer
case was settled.

412
00:21:25,251 --> 00:21:28,163
- We-We put it in writing.
- Oh.

413
00:21:31,557 --> 00:21:34,369
Well, that's, uh...
that's Peter the Penguin.

414
00:21:34,393 --> 00:21:36,505
- He's bucking for a raise.
- Raise.

415
00:21:36,529 --> 00:21:38,840
Listen, I got a meeting, so...

416
00:21:38,864 --> 00:21:40,542
Uh, uh, one-one-one other thing.

417
00:21:40,566 --> 00:21:43,845
Uh, the night Mr... Uh,
Eugene... was killed,

418
00:21:43,869 --> 00:21:46,248
uh, you-you happen to
remember where you were?

419
00:21:46,272 --> 00:21:47,449
Oh, yeah. I was in my office.

420
00:21:47,473 --> 00:21:49,084
I was trying to catch
up on all the work

421
00:21:49,108 --> 00:21:51,219
that I didn't get to because
of the settlement hearing.

422
00:21:51,243 --> 00:21:52,387
Uh-huh.

423
00:21:52,411 --> 00:21:54,723
And no, nobody saw me.

424
00:21:54,747 --> 00:21:56,792
- Ah.
- The park was closed.

425
00:21:56,816 --> 00:21:59,027
Hey, that pass I gave
you is good indefinitely,

426
00:21:59,051 --> 00:22:00,962
so, uh, come back anytime.

427
00:22:00,986 --> 00:22:02,898
Oh, well, thanks. I will.

428
00:22:02,922 --> 00:22:04,185
Yeah.

429
00:22:15,067 --> 00:22:18,780
I believe this is one
of the best hot dogs

430
00:22:18,804 --> 00:22:21,103
I've ever put in my mouth.

431
00:22:23,542 --> 00:22:25,987
I thought you said to meet
you at the white-water ride.

432
00:22:26,011 --> 00:22:27,556
- I got hungry.
- I figured.

433
00:22:27,580 --> 00:22:29,658
I believe this is one
of the best hot dogs

434
00:22:29,682 --> 00:22:31,593
I've ever put in my mouth.

435
00:22:31,617 --> 00:22:33,795
Are we gonna talk about
hot dogs all afternoon?

436
00:22:48,934 --> 00:22:50,766
Well, I'm not dead.

437
00:22:53,305 --> 00:22:55,784
I think I know how Eugene Dobbs

438
00:22:55,808 --> 00:22:58,920
gets so many personal
injury cases from here.

439
00:22:58,944 --> 00:22:59,921
How?

440
00:22:59,945 --> 00:23:03,925
Well, I think somebody who
works here for Al DeMont

441
00:23:03,949 --> 00:23:06,828
also works for Eugene Dobbs.

442
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:07,996
Any idea who?

443
00:23:08,020 --> 00:23:08,997
No.

444
00:23:09,021 --> 00:23:12,334
But I saw a sign
at the entrance.

445
00:23:12,358 --> 00:23:15,487
Said, "Help wanted."

446
00:23:18,397 --> 00:23:20,389
Have fun.

447
00:23:46,926 --> 00:23:48,103
Hey, little girl.

448
00:23:48,127 --> 00:23:49,671
What's your name?

449
00:23:49,695 --> 00:23:51,339
Elizabeth.

450
00:23:51,363 --> 00:23:53,375
Did you lose your
mommy and daddy?

451
00:23:53,399 --> 00:23:54,890
No.

452
00:24:08,280 --> 00:24:10,272
Well, Les, what happened?

453
00:24:13,252 --> 00:24:15,196
Was I right, or was I right?

454
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:17,265
That sideswiping
I told you about?

455
00:24:17,289 --> 00:24:20,435
I went to a doctor, and he
took an X-ray of my neck.

456
00:24:20,459 --> 00:24:21,770
Did he find anything?

457
00:24:21,794 --> 00:24:24,306
No. Whiplash doesn't
show up on an X-ray.

458
00:24:24,330 --> 00:24:26,141
But he told me
to wear this thing

459
00:24:26,165 --> 00:24:27,509
to be on the safe side.

460
00:24:27,533 --> 00:24:29,544
- Is there any pain?
- Yeah.

461
00:24:29,568 --> 00:24:31,646
And you know who's
gonna feel the pain?

462
00:24:31,670 --> 00:24:33,381
The guy that sideswiped me.

463
00:24:33,405 --> 00:24:35,984
It's a good thing we
exchanged license numbers.

464
00:24:36,008 --> 00:24:39,487
Well, Les, if there's no
pain, why go through with it?

465
00:24:39,511 --> 00:24:40,889
Why?

466
00:24:40,913 --> 00:24:44,459
Why?! What if I have to wear
this thing the rest of my life?

467
00:24:44,483 --> 00:24:48,396
Well, it... it doesn't
look... too bad.

468
00:24:48,420 --> 00:24:51,533
It kind of gives you
a... an aristocratic look.

469
00:24:51,557 --> 00:24:56,037
A-A-And think of the
money you'll save on ties.

470
00:24:56,061 --> 00:24:57,439
Very funny.

471
00:24:57,463 --> 00:24:59,975
You could be the
resident comedian

472
00:24:59,999 --> 00:25:01,943
at the orthopedic hospital.

473
00:25:01,967 --> 00:25:03,945
Well, Les, I'm
just trying to say,

474
00:25:03,969 --> 00:25:05,947
if the doctor didn't
find anything,

475
00:25:05,971 --> 00:25:07,983
and it doesn't hurt,
why not forget it?

476
00:25:08,007 --> 00:25:10,285
Because I want to
teach this guy a lesson.

477
00:25:10,309 --> 00:25:14,155
Sideswiping is a crime,
and I'm gonna make him pay.

478
00:25:14,179 --> 00:25:17,392
In fact, I'm on my way to
look at some sports cars.

479
00:25:17,416 --> 00:25:19,594
Uh, that's for
after I get paid off.

480
00:25:19,618 --> 00:25:22,564
You keep getting sideswiped,
you can start a collection.

481
00:25:22,588 --> 00:25:23,965
Oh, you're a scream.

482
00:25:23,989 --> 00:25:25,981
A regular Bob Hopeless.

483
00:25:32,664 --> 00:25:34,909
So, you were
working in the hallway

484
00:25:34,933 --> 00:25:37,979
outside the offices of
Wheeler & Dobbs that night?

485
00:25:38,003 --> 00:25:40,849
Yes, sir. I was vacuuming.

486
00:25:40,873 --> 00:25:42,784
What did you see?

487
00:25:42,808 --> 00:25:44,386
I saw that man right there

488
00:25:44,410 --> 00:25:46,988
get out of the elevator and
head towards their offices.

489
00:25:47,012 --> 00:25:48,156
Let the record show

490
00:25:48,180 --> 00:25:49,491
that the witness
pointed to the defendant.

491
00:25:49,515 --> 00:25:50,825
Do you recall what time it was?

492
00:25:50,849 --> 00:25:51,993
About 7:00.

493
00:25:52,017 --> 00:25:53,461
I vacuum the floors

494
00:25:53,485 --> 00:25:55,397
every night between
6:30 and 7:00.

495
00:25:55,421 --> 00:25:58,033
You're positive the man
you saw was the defendant?

496
00:25:58,057 --> 00:25:59,601
He was walking with a cane

497
00:25:59,625 --> 00:26:02,370
and wearing a red
Baxter Bulldogs cap.

498
00:26:02,394 --> 00:26:04,339
I'm a Bulldogs fan myself.

499
00:26:04,363 --> 00:26:06,730
Nothing further, Your Honor.

500
00:26:08,333 --> 00:26:09,911
Mr. Matlock?

501
00:26:09,935 --> 00:26:15,950
Uh... Now, uh... let's see.

502
00:26:15,974 --> 00:26:20,355
Uh, how far would
you say you were

503
00:26:20,379 --> 00:26:23,691
from the elevator when
you saw this person?

504
00:26:23,715 --> 00:26:25,693
About 30, 35 feet.

505
00:26:25,717 --> 00:26:26,861
Uh-huh.

506
00:26:26,885 --> 00:26:28,596
And when he was walking

507
00:26:28,620 --> 00:26:30,698
to, uh, the offices
of Wheeler & Dobbs,

508
00:26:30,722 --> 00:26:32,934
was he walking towards
you or away from you?

509
00:26:32,958 --> 00:26:34,736
- Away from me.
- Uh-huh.

510
00:26:34,760 --> 00:26:37,138
Now, you were... you were...
you were vacuuming the floors?

511
00:26:37,162 --> 00:26:38,306
That's right.

512
